From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] net: phy: maxio: drop the PHY-side RGMII delay programming (no such register)

Patch `net-phy-add-maxio-mae0621a-driver` carries Maxio's own driver, whose config_init ORs TX_DELAY
(bits 6,7) and RX_DELAY (bits 4,5) into page 0xd96 register 0 for the
rgmii-*id* phy-modes. On 2026-07-21 that register was read back on silicon
(Radxa Cubie A7S, 8 GiB, mainline 6.18.36) through a clause-22 transaction
driven by hand over MAC_MDIO_Address/MAC_MDIO_Data, after the driver had run:

	page 0xd96 reg 0x00 = 0x00001140

Bits 6, 8 and 12 set; bit 7 clear and both alleged RX bits clear. The OR would
have produced 0x11f0, so the write does not reach the PHY. It has never had an
effect, on any boot of this board.

The interesting question is not why the write is lost but whether it should be
there at all, and the datasheet answers that: it should not.

The MAE0621A has no RGMII delay register. Its register map is complete and
short -- DS v1.1 Table 8-2 "Register Mapping and Definitions" plus the MMD map
in Table 8-3 -- and lists page 0 (the IEEE clause-22 set), 0xA42 (INER), 0xA43
(PHYCR1/2, PHYSR, INSR, PAGSR), 0xA46 (PHYSCR), 0xD04 (LCR, EEELCR), 0xD08
(MIICR), 0xD40 (INTBCR) and MMD devices 3 and 7 (PCS control/status, EEE).
Page 0xd96 does not appear anywhere in the document, and none of the registers
that do appear controls a clock delay. The word "delay" occurs in the datasheet
in exactly two places outside the AC timing tables, and both are the same
thing: the hardware straps TXDLY (pin 24) and RXDLY (pin 25), sampled at reset,
each adding a fixed 2 ns (DS Table 6-8 "Hardware Configuration Interface" and
section 7.9 Table 7-3 "CONFIG Pins, Configuration Function Definition").

So on this part the RGMII delay is board-set, not software-set, and on this
board the straps are already known from the schematic (SCH rev V1.10 page 15):

	RXDLY  = RXD0/pin25, R120 4.7k to VCCIO_PHY, R119 NC  ->  1, 2 ns on RXC
	TXDLY  = RXD1/pin24, R121 4.7k pull-down, R122 NC     ->  0, no TX delay

The silicon readback agrees with that reading and disagrees with the driver's:
bit 6, which the driver calls half of TX_DELAY, is *set* although TXDLY is
strapped 0, and both bits the driver calls RX_DELAY are *clear* although RXDLY
is strapped 1. Whatever page 0xd96 register 0 is, its bits do not mean what the
symbol names in `net-phy-add-maxio-mae0621a-driver` claim.

Making that write land would therefore mean programming an undocumented
register with guessed bit semantics, on a link that currently passes traffic.
Delete the block instead. The switch statement, the two defines, the label and
the two error paths go with it, as does the `dev` variable they used.

This is a no-op on silicon by construction: the write never took effect, so
removing it cannot change the RGMII timing. In particular it does NOT
invalidate the delays measured in `arm64-dts-a733-gmac0-measured-rgmii-delays` -- those were measured with the PHY-side
write already failing, i.e. under exactly the conditions this patch makes
permanent. tx-internal-delay-ps = <700> / rx-internal-delay-ps = <1000> stay as
they are and must not be recalculated.

Also gone with the block: the `else if (ret == 0)` branch that reported "2ns
delay was already enabled/disabled (by pin-strapping RXD1 or bootloader
configuration)". ret there is the return of phy_write(), where 0 means the MDIO
write completed -- the branch fired on success, not on "already set", and would
have printed that message on every single config_init.

phy-mode stays "rgmii-id" in the DT. It describes the delay the link needs, not
which end inserts it; stmmac/dwmac-sun55i programs the MAC side from the DT
properties, and the PHY side is whatever the straps say.

Not touched, deliberately: config_init also writes page 0xd8f register 0
(auto-speed-down) and maxio_mae0621a_clk_init() touches pages 0xa43, 0xd92 and
0x0. Pages 0xd8f and 0xd92 are undocumented too, but unlike the delay block
nothing in the datasheet contradicts them, no register readback disputes them,
and no patch header of ours claims a specific effect for them. They are left
for a separate look rather than folded into this fix.

Anchors:
  DS = MAE0621A Gigabit Ethernet Transceiver datasheet v1.1 (2024-08),
       Table 6-8 (section 6.8), Table 7-3 (section 7.9), Table 8-2 (section 8.2),
       Table 8-3 (section 8.3).
  SCH = board schematic RS504 rev V1.10 page 15.
  SI  = clause-22 readback 2026-07-21, quoted in `arm64-dts-a733-gmac0-measured-rgmii-delays`.

Verified: applies clean on the full series over pristine 6.18.36, and
drivers/net/phy/maxio.o builds with W=1 KCFLAGS=-Werror. NOT verified on
hardware.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/net/phy/maxio.c
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/maxio.c
@@ -20,8 +20,6 @@
 #define MAXIO_MAE0621A_INER				0x12
 #define MAXIO_MAE0621A_INER_LINK_STATUS	BIT(4)
 #define MAXIO_MAE0621A_INSR				0x1d
-#define MAXIO_MAE0621A_TX_DELAY			(BIT(6)|BIT(7))
-#define MAXIO_MAE0621A_RX_DELAY			(BIT(4)|BIT(5))
 #define MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_PAGE			0xd04
 #define MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR			0x10
 #define MAXIO_MAE0621A_LED1_LINK_1000		BIT(8)
@@ -104,8 +102,6 @@
 
 static int maxio_mae0621a_config_init(struct phy_device *phydev)
 {
-	struct device *dev = &phydev->mdio.dev;
-	u16 val;
 	int ret;
 
 	maxio_mae0621a_clk_init(phydev);
@@ -120,41 +116,23 @@
 
 	//enable auto_speed_down
 	ret = maxio_write_paged(phydev, 0xd8f, 0x0, 0x300 );
- 
-	//adjust TX/RX delay
-	switch (phydev->interface) {
-	case PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_RGMII:
-		val = 0x0;
-		break;
-	case PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_RGMII_ID:
-		val = MAXIO_MAE0621A_TX_DELAY | MAXIO_MAE0621A_RX_DELAY;
-		break;
-	case PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_RGMII_RXID:
-		val = MAXIO_MAE0621A_RX_DELAY;
-		break;
-	case PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_RGMII_TXID:
-		val = MAXIO_MAE0621A_TX_DELAY;
-		break;
-	default: /* the rest of the modes imply leaving delays as is. */
-		goto delay_skip;
-	}
-
-	ret = maxio_read_paged(phydev, 0xd96, 0x0);
-	if (ret < 0) {
-		dev_err(dev, "Failed to update the TX delay register\n");
-		return ret;
-	}
 
-	ret =maxio_write_paged(phydev, 0xd96, 0x0, val|ret );
-	if (ret < 0) {
-		dev_err(dev, "Failed to update the TX delay register\n");
-		return ret;
-	} else if (ret == 0) {
-		dev_dbg(dev,
-			"2ns  delay was already %s (by pin-strapping RXD1 or bootloader configuration)\n",
-			val ? "enabled" : "disabled");
-	}
-delay_skip:
+	/*
+	 * No PHY-side RGMII delay is programmed here, and that is deliberate.
+	 *
+	 * The MAE0621A has no RGMII delay register. Its complete register map --
+	 * DS v1.1 Table 8-2 "Register Mapping and Definitions" plus the MMD map
+	 * Table 8-3 -- covers pages 0, 0xA42, 0xA43, 0xA46, 0xD04, 0xD08, 0xD40
+	 * and MMD devices 3 and 7, and none of those registers controls a delay.
+	 * The only delay control the datasheet documents is a pair of hardware
+	 * straps sampled at reset: TXDLY on pin 24 and RXDLY on pin 25 (DS Table
+	 * 6-8 and section 7.9 Table 7-3), each adding a fixed 2 ns.
+	 *
+	 * On the Cubie A7S those straps are RXDLY = 1 (PHY delays RXC by 2 ns)
+	 * and TXDLY = 0 (no PHY TX delay), so the MAC has to supply the TX side.
+	 * It does: the board DT carries the measured
+	 * tx-internal-delay-ps / rx-internal-delay-ps in its gmac0 node.
+	 */
 
 	phy_write(phydev, MII_BMCR, BMCR_RESET | phy_read(phydev, MII_BMCR));
 	msleep(1);
